"```python\nimport json\n\ndef serialize_object(obj, indent=0):\n    \"\"\"Recursively serialize an object into a JSON string.\"\"\"\n    if isinstance(obj, dict):\n        return '{' + ', '.join(f'\"{key}\": {serialize_object(value, indent + 2)}' for key, value in obj.items()) + '}'\n    elif isinstance(obj, list):\n        return '[' + ', '.join(serialize_object(item, indent + 2) for item in obj) + ']'\n    elif hasattr(obj, '__dict__'):\n        return '{' + ', '.join(f'\"{key}\": {serialize_object(value, indent + 2)}' for key, value in obj.__dict__.items()) + '}'\n    else:\n        return str(obj)\n\n# Example usage:\nobj = {\n    'name': 'John',\n    'age': 30,\n    'interests': ['reading', 'hiking', 'coding'],\n    'address': {\n        'street': '123 Main St',\n        'city': 'Anytown',\n        'state': 'CA',\n        'zip': '12345'\n    }\n}\n\njson_string = json.dumps(serialize_object(obj), indent=4)\nprint(json_string)\n```"
"```python\nimport hashlib\nimport base64\nimport os\nimport secrets\n\nclass SCRAM:\n    def __init__(self, salt, password, algorithm='argon2i', iterations=4, key_length=32):\n        self.salt = salt\n        self.password = password\n        self.algorithm = algorithm\n        self.iterations = iterations\n        self.key_length = key_length\n\n    def _hash(self, password, salt, iterations, algorithm):\n        if algorithm == 'argon2i':\n            import argon2\n            return argon2.hash(password, salt, iterations=iterations)\n        elif algorithm == 'pbkdf2':\n            import hashlib\n            return hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac('sha256', password.encode('utf-8'), salt.encode('utf-8'), iterations=iterations)\n        else:\n            raise ValueError(\"Unsupported algorithm\")\n\n    def _derive_key(self, password, salt, iterations, algorithm):\n        return self._hash(password, salt, iterations, algorithm).hex()\n\n    def _authenticate(self, password, salt, derived_key):\n        return self._hash(password, salt, self.iterations, self.algorithm) == derived_key\n\n    def authenticate(self, password, salt, derived_key):\n        return self._authenticate(password, salt, derived_key)\n\n    def derive_key(self, password, salt):\n        return self._derive_key(password, salt, self.iterations, self.algorithm)\n\n    def _generate_salt(self):\n        return secrets.token_bytes(16)\n\n    def generate_salt(self):\n        return self._generate_salt()\n\n    def password_verify(self, password, salt, derived_key):\n        return self.authenticate(password, salt, derived_key)\n\n    def verify(self, password, salt, derived_key):\n        return self.password_verify(password, salt, derived_key)\n\n\n# Example usage:\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    scram = SCRAM(_generate_salt(), \"mysecretpassword\")\n    salt = scram.generate_salt()\n    derived_key = scram.derive_key(\"mysecretpassword\", salt)\n    print(scram.authenticate(\"mysecretpassword\", salt, derived_key))\n    print(scram.verify(\"mysecretpassword\", salt, derived_key))\n```\n\nThis Python code implements the SCRAM protocol for password-based authentication. The SCRAM (Server-Client Authentication and Key Exchange with Passwords) protocol is a password-based authentication protocol that is used to securely authenticate users with their passwords. \n\nThis code supports multiple hash functions (argon2i and pbkdf2) and encryption methods. The `SCRAM` class takes a salt, password, algorithm, iterations, and key length as input parameters. The `generate_salt` method generates a random salt. The `derive_key` method derives a key from the password and salt. The `authenticate` method authenticates the password against the derived key. The `verify` method verifies the password against the derived key."
