"```python\nimport ctypes\n\n# Load the C++11 library\nlib = ctypes.CDLL('./cpp_library.so')\n\n# Define a class in Python that wraps the C++ class\nclass CppClass:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.cpp_obj = lib.CppClass()\n\n    def get_value(self):\n        return self.cpp_obj.get_value()\n\n    def set_value(self, value):\n        self.cpp_obj.set_value(value)\n\n# Create an instance of the C++ class\ncpp_obj = CppClass()\n\n# Call the C++ function\nresult = cpp_obj.get_value()\nprint(result)\n\n# Modify the C++ object's value\ncpp_obj.set_value(10)\n\n# Get the updated value\nresult = cpp_obj.get_value()\nprint(result)\n```\n\nNote: This code assumes you have a shared library `cpp_library.so` with the C++11 class `CppClass` implemented in it. The actual library name and implementation may vary depending on your specific use case.\n\nTo compile the C++ code, you would use a command like this:\n\n```bash\ng++ -shared -o cpp_library.so -I/usr/include/c++/11 -L/usr/lib/c++/11/ -lc++ -lpthread cpp.cpp -fPIC\n```\n\nPlease replace the `cpp.cpp` file with your actual C++ file."
"```python\nimport pycodestyle\n\ndef check_style(code_path):\n    \"\"\"Check the style of the given Python code.\"\"\"\n    try:\n        result = pycodestyle.check_file(code_path)\n        return result\n    except FileNotFoundError:\n        print(f\"File not found: {code_path}\")\n        return None\n\n# Example usage\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    code_path = \"path_to_your_python_file.py\"\n    result = check_style(code_path)\n    if result is not None:\n        for line, message in result:\n            print(f\"{line}: {message}\")\n```\n\nTo use this code, replace `\"path_to_your_python_file.py\"` with the actual path to your Python file. The `check_style` function takes a file path as input and returns the result of the style check. If any violations are found, the function prints each line with the corresponding error message."
